al  relationship was so fundamental to so  much of the function of Egypt and the  Ottoman Empire in the early modern  period and in the 19th century there's a  surfeit of sources for this topic all  kinds of sources both in various  registers you know very local sources  very Imperial sources sources from  outside the empower different languages  etc so you know I enjoy the ability to  combine all of these different kinds of  source materials so let's talk a little  bit about why it's important to look at  the relationship between humans and  animals during this time period tell us  a little bit about it ok so if you think  about a place like Egypt in the 16th  17th and 18th centuries it was a rural  place in which agriculture was central  both to the kind of capital regime of  Egypt taxation to people's labouring  lives right and also to issues of  ideology and imperial sovereignty so  what do I mean by that humans relied on  animals in the countryside to plow  fields to maintain irrigation works to  provide food sometimes in the winters  animals would even brought be brought  into people's homes to as a source of  heat so if they were tied to the daily  labouring lives of rural cultivators in  a place like Egypt and in most other  parts of the world as well and therefore  because of their importance to the  laboring economy there was a lot of  money  tied up in animals so we see that  through these these estate inventories  that one animals represent the largest  share of rural wealth - that they were  involved in very complicated economic  relations so one could for example own  shares in an animal you could buy  futures in an animal Wow  you know we have cases in which someone  owns a third of a water buffalo someone  owns half and did they share it like you  have it for this week you have it for  that way so those shares represented  both sort of pro-rata rights to the  laboring time of that animal right so if  you owned a third of the water buffalo  you got it