ing is that I think it points us to  the fact that animals served really  important crucial economic function sure  in the country so when they were  dependent on their livelihood exactly  and an animal labor and so that was one  of the things that that's one of the  ways that my work sort of diverges from  some of the other work on human animal  relations that really focus on these  affective relationships and then also  kind of the philosophical implications  of thinking about animals as historical  subjects  I was really interested in this book in  the kind of productive laboring and  economic functions of animals that said  dogs do play a role in my book a big  role so I have two chapters on ok so  tell us about the dogs ok so I'm going  to make me cry so the first thing to say  is that it's very common these sort of  the the commonplace thing that you hear  is that in Muslim societies in Islam  dogs are considered virtually impurity  read even from the time of the Prophet  himself so there's a there's a body of  literature that is a collection of the  sayings and doings of the Prophet as  related by his companions ok ok so if  you read and this is a very important  body of literature in in the scholarship  on the history of Islam if you read the  even that body of literature there are  some accounts that say the Prophet  himself prayed in the presence of dogs  some of his companions kept dogs that  actually if a Muslim saw a dog while  praying it would not invalidate his or  her prayer and then there are there are  accounts that contradict that but the  important thing to say is that it's a  mixed bag it's not all bad it's not all  good but it's also not all bad mm-hmm ok  so that's the first point to make moving  on from you know the times of the  Prophet if you know so thinking about  literature produced between the 10th and  the 12th century about dogs we have lots  of accounts about the wonderful  attributes of dogs their loyalty their  trustworthiness dogs