s you realize  that  there's just an enormous amount of  material on this topic and I only  scratched the surface I mean there are  various things that you know that  there's just so you know too much for  one person to undertake and and you know  I think and when one of the points that  I made in the book is that this should  not be a surprise to us given the  centrality of animals to all these  laboring and economic and social and  political functions that I've gotten  into and that we should we should take  this relationship seriously because it's  of enormous historical importance so I  think that was sort of the main thing  that that surprised me in doing okay so  so basically what do you conclude in the  book okay so the main thing that I want  readers to take away from the book is to  understand that the human animal  relationship is historically contingent  and that it changed over time and the  specific change that I'm interested in  tracking over the course of my book is a  transition around the turn of the 19th  century so roughly from let's say you  know 1750 to 1850 and this is a  transition in the human animal  relationship and it's a transition in  the kind of economic history and  political history of Egypt's place in  the Ottoman Empire and this is a  function of a more global transition  that lots of societies are undertaking  and this has usually been glossed as  something like commercialization or the  age of revolutions capitalism and so  that those kinds of transitions are also  happening in Egypt and for me the  human-animal relationship that the  economic role of animals and how that  changes is part and parcel of that story  so I want to understand how the  dialectic relationship between those  those processes capitalism  commercialization modernization state  formation and the human animal  relationship and the general story in a  very broad brushstroke that I tell is  one of  of going from a society in which human  animal relations are integral to economy  society politics to one in which they're  no longer central one in which animals  are no longer the the the major motoring  force of society right a world in which  dogs are no longer the main means of  public hygiene in in urban societies a  world in which these charismatic  megafauna are used in very different  ways and that story is one of the  emergence of the zoo as an institution  and kind of the modern trade in animals  which again is it's big business and  continues to this day so again going  from a world in which there's kind of an  integral relationship between humans and  animals and all these different spheres  into one in which animals are still  integral but in which they are there  they are no longer at the center of  society and become marginalized in  various kinds of ways and so what are  the economic political and social  implications of that marginalization  very good fascinating actually thank you  so much for being here today with us and  sharing some of your work for more  information about Professor Mikhail and  his work please visit our website at  Yale edu slash Macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale

