 has you know  spent time in a park or walking in New  York City will see that the the human  dog relationship is a very important one  you know it's a billion-dollar industry  yes if we think about something like  meat production in the United States all  of these things will see that that you  know humans and animals have been locked  into these very important and mutually  constitutive relationships in all places  and in all times so you know I'm I did a  very small slice of this you know in my  own area of expertise which was the  Ottoman Empire in Egypt in the early  modern period now let me ask you this  you mentioned dogs were their pets in  the not so much in in the upper echelon  of society although I imagine they were  there as well but you know in  agricultural the rural households did  they have pets that's a great question  so you know for modern Americans most of  us do not live on farms and most of our  interactions with animals let's say  beyond consuming them as food is what  historians and people in animal studies  called an effective or companionate  relationship right so pet keeping right  so interestingly and there's been a lot  of literature on this relationship on  how its formed on ideas of domesticity  and how they get played out in this  relationship on what it means for  notions of humanity of human rights and  of animal rights and kind of the border  between humans and other animals in my  case in the countryside in the 16th 17th  and 18th centuries I don't find much  evidence of an effective relationship  between humans and animals and other  animals we don't have a lot of examples  of pet keeping in the countryside and  that's curious and I and I think it's I  think that's important for two reasons  one it shows us that that effective  relationship between humans and other  animals it is a historical relationship  that has to be contextualized it did not  always exist so we have to understand  when it emerged why and where the other  thing is that I think it points us to  the fact that animals served really  important crucial economic function sure  in the country so when they were  dependent on their livelihood exactly  and an animal labor and so that was one  of the things that that's one of the  ways that my work sort of diverges from  some of the other work on human animal  relations that really focus on these  affective relationships and then also  kind of the philosophical implications  of thinking about animals as historical  subjects  I was really interested in this book in  the kind of productive laboring and  economic functions of animals that said  dogs do play a role in my book a big  role so I have two chapters on ok so  tell us about the dogs ok so I'm going  to make me cry so the first thing to say  is that it's very common these sort of  the the commonplace thing that you hear  is that in Muslim societies in Islam  dogs are considered virtually impurity  read even from the time of the Prophet  himself so there's a there's a body of  literature that is a collection of the  sayings and doings of the Prophet as  related by his companions ok ok so if  you read and this is a very important  body of literature in in the scholarship  on the history of Islam if you read the  even that body of literature there are  some accounts that say the Prophet  himself prayed in the presence of dogs  some of his companions kept dogs that  actually if a Muslim saw a dog while  praying it would not invalidate his or  her prayer and then there are there are  accounts that contradict that but the  important thing to say is that it's a  mixed bag it's not all bad it's not all  good but it's also not all bad mm-hmm ok  so that's the first point to make moving  on from you know the times of the  Prophet if you know so thinking about  literature produced between the 10th and  the 12th century about dogs we have lots  of accounts about the wonderful  attributes of dogs their loyalty their  trustworthiness dogs sacrificing to save  humans they're productive functions for  controlling herds for providing security  and cities so again a very a very  positive take on the presence of dogs in  society and very little actually about  their impurity or  that there are negative presence in  society they also serves a very  productive function in major cities in  the Middle East and you know some of the  oldest cities in the world are in the  Middle East and the main productive  function that they served beyond  security was consuming garbage  so they actually served a sort of Public  Health and hygiene function by eating  garbage in the cities and we see this in  other cities around the world in various  periods of dogs and other animals  consuming garbage in cities so in New  York City in the nineteenth century for  example hogs were very important for  keeping streets clean and and so in the  ottoman period for example in Cairo and  in Istanbul and in other cities we have  accounts of Ottoman authorities in  cities actually putting out food for  dogs providing water troughs for them  court cases of punishing people who  committed violence against dogs so again  a recognition that dogs played an  integral role in human societies and  that that role was important and had to  be supported so that's the good part of  the story this that there's a transition  in this relationship between humans and  dogs and specific in that in the early  19th century various processes came to  change this relationship one of them was  increasing urbanization and increasing  urban populations so there was a thing  about Cairo and specific here there was  a a pressure placed on on space inside  the city and as part of that garbage was  moved outside of the city okay as part  of this process that the second thing  that sort of reinforced this change in  the human dog relationship it was a  changing ideas about disease and where  disease came from so the early 19th  century in Cairo this is the period in  which we begin to see ideas that disease  came from rotting rotting  rotting entities that produced this bad  air right and that breathing that air  would cause one to get sick so these  writing entities included garbage they  could be dank ponds swampy areas rotting  flesh this is also a moment in which  cemeteries are moved outside of Cairo so  moving the garbage mounds outside of the  city meant that the sort of integral  role that dogs played of consuming this  garbage was no longer exigent there was  no longer a need for dogs inside the  city to consume this garbage because the  garbage had been moved outside of the  city and those former places were now  made into homes and businesses and other  things there and so this means that also  in this period there's a shift towards a  different solution to the problem of  urban sanitation and garbage collection  in that there were certain urban  populations of humans who became the  main garbage collectors in Cairo in this  period so a kind of if you like  replacement of the former labouring role  of dogs in taking care of garbage inside  the city moving from dogs to humans  right okay so in doing in doing your  research did you find anything that was  particularly surprising that you weren't  anticipating well I mean I think just  the sheer sort of presence of different  classes of animals and we've we barely  touched on the third class of animals  that I was interested in charismatic  megafauna okay which were the lions and  tigers and elephants and giraffes and  things that were a part of sovereignty  and the hunt and those sorts of things  so I was just sort of really impressed  with the sheer amount of archival  material about animals I mean this is  not a topic that has been worked on very  much in Ottoman history and in the  Middle East I mean they're they're some  important important works but for the  most part you know this topic has been  ignored but you know when you when you  start reading the sources you realize  that  there's just an enormous amount of  material on this topic and I only  scratched the surface I mean there are  various things that you know that  there's just so you know too much for  one person to undertake and and you know  I think and when one of the points that  I made in the book is that this should  not be a surprise to us given the  centrality of animals to all these  laboring and economic and social and  political functions that I've gotten  into and that we should we should take  this relationship seriously because it's  of enormous historical importance so I  think that was sort of the main thing  that that surprised me in doing okay so  so basically what do you conclude in the  book okay so the main thing that I want  readers to take away from the book is to  understand that the human animal  relationship is historically contingent  and that it changed over time and the  specific change that I'm interested in  tracking over the course of my book is a  transition around the turn of the 19th  century so roughly from let's say you  know 1750 to 1850 and this is a  transition in the human animal  relationship and it's a transition in  the kind of economic history and  political history of Egypt's place in  the Ottoman Empire and this is a  function of a more global transition  that lots of societies are undertaking  and this has usually been glossed as  something like commercialization or the  age of revolutions capitalism and so  that those kinds of transitions are also  happening in Egypt and for me the  human-animal relationship that the  economic role of animals and how that  changes is part and parcel of that story  so I want to understand how the  dialectic relationship between those  those processes capitalism  commercialization modernization state  formation and the human animal  relationship and the general story in a  very broad brushstroke that I tell is  one of  of going from a society in which human  animal relations are integral to economy  society politics to one in which they're  no longer central one in which animals  are no longer the the the major motoring  force of society right a world in which  dogs are no longer the main means of  public hygiene in in urban societies a  world in which these charismatic  megafauna are used in very different  ways and that story is one of the  emergence of the zoo as an institution  and kind of the modern trade in animals  which again is it's big business and  continues to this day so again going  from a world in which there's kind of an  integral relationship between humans and  animals and all these different spheres  into one in which animals are still  integral but in which they are there  they are no longer at the center of  society and become marginalized in  various kinds of ways and so what are  the economic political and social  implications of that marginalization  very good fascinating actually thank you  so much for being here today with us and  sharing some of your work for more  information about Professor Mikhail and  his work please visit our website at  Yale edu slash Macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale
 

